---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Editorial Team | 1rmaxcalculator.com';
const description = 'Meet the editorial team behind 1rmaxcalculator.com — strength training specialists who verify calculator formulas, review programming guides, and maintain accuracy standards.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Editorial Team</h1>
  <p>1rmaxcalculator.com is written and reviewed by a small editorial team at The Click Lab. Every calculator's underlying math is verified against published research before publication, and every guide goes through editorial review to ensure accuracy, appropriate caveats, and alignment with current strength training evidence.</p>

  <h2>Our editorial team</h2>
  <p>Our editorial team reviews all calculator methodology documentation, percentage-based programming content, and peaking block guides, drawing on NSCA Certified Strength and Conditioning Specialist (CSCS) expertise and over a decade of experience working with competitive powerlifters and strength athletes at the amateur and masters levels, including the intersection of autoregulation and percentage-based programming — building systems that account for day-to-day performance variability while maintaining long-term progressive overload. Content is reviewed for technical accuracy against NSCA position statements and peer-reviewed exercise science literature. The team also reviews content related to macronutrient calculation, nutrition periodization for strength athletes, and recovery protocols, drawing on Precision Nutrition Level 1 (PN1) certification and nutrition coaching experience with strength sport athletes — primarily powerlifters, Olympic weightlifters, and competitive CrossFit athletes — covering the practical application of nutrition science to strength training: how protein targets shift across training blocks, how carbohydrate periodization interacts with intensity cycles, and how recovery nutrition affects the week-to-week performance patterns that underlie good percentage-based programming.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Formula sourcing.</strong> Every formula used in a calculator is cited to its primary research publication. We do not use proprietary or undocumented calculation methods.</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy first.</strong> When research conflicts or methodology improves, we update the content. Update dates are noted on each page.</li>
    <li><strong>No training or medical prescriptions.</strong> Our content is informational and educational. Specific training decisions should involve a qualified strength coach; medical decisions should involve a licensed healthcare provider. We do not provide individualized training plans or medical advice.</li>
    <li><strong>No paid placements.</strong> We do not accept money to recommend programs, equipment, supplements, or services. Recommendations are editorially independent.</li>
    <li><strong>Appropriate caveats.</strong> Calculator outputs are estimates, not measurements. Every calculator page notes the expected accuracy range and the conditions under which the estimate is and isn't reliable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections policy</h2>
  <p>If you find a factual error in a calculator, formula, or guide — whether a mathematical mistake, an outdated research reference, or a claim that doesn't match the evidence — please email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a> with the page URL and the specific error. Verified corrections are made within a few business days. Significant corrections are noted at the bottom of the affected page with the correction date.</p>

  <h2>About The Click Lab</h2>
  <p>1rmaxcalculator.com is operated by The Click Lab, a publisher of free-to-use calculation tools in legal, financial, health, and fitness categories. The site is supported by Google AdSense advertising and has no paid relationships with product vendors or supplement companies. Editorial content is kept entirely separate from advertising decisions.</p>
</BaseLayout>
